> I see no reason not to expect the rule to apply to roguelikes as well.

The thing I've always thought as making something Like Rogue has been
item scrambling and ID.  I'm starting to consider devoting a later
column to the item ID/unified movement & combat thing, however --
obviously, people think strongly about it.

Back to defending my argument:
There are games that contain that but don't have unified movement and
combat that have the feel of Rogue -- the dungeon game in Tobal No. 2
being one of them, it seems.  There are *many* games with unified
movement and combat that do not have its feel -- Diablo and Gauntlet
being good examples.

(I also disagree with Sturgeon's Law.  It's a rule of thumb that is far
too often treated as if it were an immutable law of creative works.  It
might be an immutable law about perceptions, but I doubt the people who
invoke it would describe it that way.)


